<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Caereni</h1><p class="st-meta">100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">People of the far north-west of Scotland, named only by Ptolemy, living on the Atlantic coast of Sutherland.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Caereni appear in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Ptolemy's Geography</a> of about 150 on the north-western mainland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, north of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/carnonacae">Carnonacae</a>, usually located in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutherland">Sutherland</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assynt">Assynt</a> and Kinlochbervie. Their name has been interpreted as meaning 'sheep folk', though this is uncertain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nothing is recorded of their rulers or institutions. The Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutherland">Sutherland</a> was a thinly peopled land of rock, bog and sea lochs, and its inhabitants lived in roundhouses and brochs, herding livestock, fishing and farming small patches of better land.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Caereni are not mentioned again. Their region later formed part of the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> and from the 9th century came under the control of Norse settlers and the earls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/caereni.jpg" alt="Caereni" width="320" height="464"><figcaption>Image: (myself) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britain.north.peoples.Ptolemy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic (Pictish)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=caereni" aria-label="Caereni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4639 321 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Caereni lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4959 577h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4959 577h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United Kingdom. <a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=caereni">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

